    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This paper shows that short selling is an important component in the trading\r\nof initial public offerings. The finding goes against conventional wisdom in the IPO\r\nliterature. Short sellers are informed traders who trade on negative information. Since their trades provide signals to the market, the short interest ratio serves as an indicator of subsequent low returns on a stock. This paper studies data on IPOs in the\r\ntechnology sector issued on the Nasdaq and NYSE between October 2007 and\r\nDecember 2015. The multiple regression analysis in this paper evaluates the\r\nperformance of returns on tech IPOs based on many variables, including book-to-market ratio, market premium, and firm size. The results provide evidence that the\r\naverage tech IPO exhibits a negative relationship between short interest ratio and\r\nabnormal monthly returns during the first year of trading.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
